\begin_body

\begin_layout Section
Classical Information and Quantum Information
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Classical Information
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Single Bits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A bit (short for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

binary digit
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) is the informational element in computing.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A bit can have a value of either 0 or 1:
 
\begin_inset Formula $b\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Usually,
 we need a physical system to encode the bit
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Examples:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
a switch might be open for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and closed for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A ray of light might have a vertical polarization (
\begin_inset Formula $\updownarrow$
\end_inset

) or a horizontal polarization (
\begin_inset Formula $\leftrightarrow$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
An electron might have an upward spin (
\begin_inset Formula $\uparrow$
\end_inset

,
 "spin up") or a downward spin (
\begin_inset Formula $\downarrow$
\end_inset

,
 "spin down")
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We call these basic binary states the 
\series bold
classical states
\series default
 of a system
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In the context of quantum mechanics,
 we call them the 
\series bold
classical basis
\series default
 states or the 
\series bold
computational basis
\series default
 states.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection
Multiple Bits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can combine several bits,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(b_{0},b_{1},\ldots,b_{n-1})$
\end_inset

 into an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-bit word,
 which we give the symbol 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{b}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{b}=b_{n-1}b_{n-2}\cdots b_{1}b_{0}=b_{0}2^{0}+b_{1}2^{1}+\cdots=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}b_{k}2^{k}=x
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Here,
 we're thinking of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{b}$
\end_inset

 as a binary vector,
 and we are associating it with an integer 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Classical States - Revisited
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Previously,
 we used 0 and 1 to representing two states of a physical system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Now,
 we must expand mathematical notation to support quantum information.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
To do this,
 we first transition to a quantum notation for the two 
\series bold
classical
\series default
 states,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|0\right>$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|1\right>$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\right>$
\end_inset

 denotes the state of a quantum system and is called a 
\series bold
ket
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The symbol inside the ket denotes the state.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|0\right>$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|1\right>$
\end_inset

,
 indicate states of the quantum system that map to the classical device states.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Now,
 however,
 we assign these states not a bit,
 but rather a two dimensional vector:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|0\right>\leftrightarrow\begin{bmatrix}1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\left|1\right>\leftrightarrow\begin{bmatrix}0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}\label{eq:ElementaryBasisKets}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 we have two realms here:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
one is the physical world,
 in which we have states of a quantum system denoted by a ket;
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the other world is a mathematical one,
 where each ket is represented by a vector
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Vectors and their transformations are within the realm of linear algebra
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For some basic linear algebra notes,
 please see Appendix 
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Linear-Algebra"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Since these vectors are associated with a physical state,
 we call them 
\series bold
state vectors
\series default
,
 or just 
\series bold
states
\series default
.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
There is nothing quantum yet about the classical states,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|0\right>$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|1\right>$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We will use them to make something quantum next.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Something New:
 a quantum state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Now,
 we can form an arbitrary quantum state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right>$
\end_inset

,
 by making a weighted sum of the classical basis states:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|\psi\right>=c_{0}\left|0\right>+c_{1}\left|1\right>\label{eq:defineQubit}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This is called a 
\series bold
quantum superposition
\series default
 of basis states
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In quantum realm,
 we have a quantum superposition of basis states.
 In linear algebra,
 a weighted sum of basis vectors is called a 
\series bold
linear combination
\series default
.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In the field of quantum computing,
 this arbitrary state 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right>$
\end_inset

 is called a 
\series bold
qubit
\series default
,
 short for 
\series bold
quantum bit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This is a quantum state,
 not possible in the classical world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The coefficients,
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 are complex numbers,
 and in quantum mechanics,
 we call them 
\series bold
probability amplitudes
\series default
,
 or simply 
\series bold
amplitudes
\series default
 for short.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If you need help with complex numbers,
 please see Appendix 
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Complex-Numbers"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The probability amplitudes allow us to calculate the probability of measuring 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 on a qubit prepared in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The probability for measuring 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\left(0\right)=\left|c_{0}\right|^{2}=c_{0}^{\ast}c_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The probability for measuring 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\left(1\right)=\left|c_{1}\right|^{2}=c_{1}^{\ast}c_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A measurement of a qubit can only produce 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 so the probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(0\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(1\right)$
\end_inset

 must sum to unity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|c_{0}\right|^{2}+\left|c_{1}\right|^{2}=1\label{eq:NormalizationCondition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This condition is called 
\series bold
normalization
\series default
,
 and meaningful quantum states require normalization
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We now pair the qubit with its linear algebraic (matrix) representation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\psi}$
\end_inset

 (sometimes,
 I also use 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\psi}$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|\psi\right>=c_{0}\left|0\right>+c_{1}\left|1\right>\qquad\leftrightarrow\qquad\vec{\psi}=c_{0}\begin{bmatrix}1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}+c_{1}\begin{bmatrix}0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{bmatrix}\label{eq:QubitAndMatrixRep}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Notice that we have expanded 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 over the computational basis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\begin{array}{cc}
c_{0} & c_{1}\end{array}\right]^{T}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\series bold
matrix representation
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
in the computational basis
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We could choose another basis over which to expand the same 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

,
 say,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{x_{0}},\ket{x_{1}}\right\} $
\end_inset

 but then we would generally have a new matrix representation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\psi\right>=d_{0}\left|x_{0}\right>+d_{1}\left|x_{1}\right>\qquad\leftrightarrow\qquad\vec{\psi}=\begin{bmatrix}d_{0}\\
d_{1}
\end{bmatrix}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We call the collection of all valid states 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{\psi}\right\} $
\end_inset

 a 
\series bold
Hilbert space
\series default
,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The states 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{0},\ket{1}\right\} $
\end_inset

 are a basis for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

,
 because any member state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 of the Hilbert space can be made from a quantum superposition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Another way to say this is that 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 span the Hilbert space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Since at least two basis states are required to span 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

,
 we call 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 a two-dimensional Hilbert space
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can scale a state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 by a non-zero and non-infinite scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and still have another member of the Hilbert space:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi^{\prime}}=\alpha\ket{\psi}=\alpha c_{0}\ket{0}+\alpha c_{1}\ket{1}\quad\leftrightarrow\quad\alpha\left[\begin{array}{c}
c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\alpha c_{0}\\
\alpha c_{1}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Scalar multiplication commutes,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\ket{\psi}=\ket{\psi}\alpha$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Inner Products
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For every ket 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

,
 there is a dual object,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bra{\psi}$
\end_inset

,
 which is called a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bra
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The dual relationship may be note by the dagger operator:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi}^{\dagger}=\bra{\psi}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The dagger operator is the Hermitian conjugate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If we apply the dagger operator to 
\begin_inset Formula $\bra{\psi},$
\end_inset

we get 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bra{\psi}^{\dagger}=\ket{\psi}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
When we dagger a scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

,
 we get the complex conjugate:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha^{\dagger}=\alpha^{\ast}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When we dagger a product,
 we apply the dagger to each factor and reverse the order
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\alpha\ket{\psi}\right)^{\dagger}=\bra{\psi}\alpha^{\ast}=\alpha^{\ast}\bra{\psi}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When we dagger a quanutm superposition,
 we dagger each term:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi}^{\dagger}=\left(c_{0}\ket{0}+c_{1}\ket{1}\right)^{\dagger}=c_{0}^{\ast}\bra{0}+c_{1}^{\ast}\bra{1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A bra and a ket form a product called a bra-ket (from the term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bracket
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\chi}$
\end_inset

 be quantum states so that they can form a braket,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\psi|\chi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\psi|\chi}$
\end_inset

 is called the inner product of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\chi}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This is a scalar indicating how similar 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\chi}$
\end_inset

 is to 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\psi|\chi}$
\end_inset

 is also called a 
\series bold
scalar product
\series default
,
 and it is equivalent to the 
\series bold
dot product
\series default
 from geometry in 2D or 3D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\psi|\chi}=0$
\end_inset

,
 the states are completely dissimilar and are said to be 
\series bold
orthogonal
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
What is a bra,
 and what is the 
\begin_inset Formula $\dagger$
\end_inset

 operator?
 Let us turn to linear algebra to understand this better
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In linear algebra,
 to take the Hermitian conjugate of a matrix is to take the conjugate and transpose:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi}^{\dagger}\qquad\leftrightarrow\qquad\left[\begin{array}{cc}
x_{0}^{\ast} & x_{1}^{\ast}\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
What is an inner product?
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\chi}$
\end_inset

 be
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\chi}=x_{0}\ket{0}+x_{1}\ket{1}\qquad\leftrightarrow\qquad\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{0}\\
x_{1}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Then,
 we can evaluate the inner product 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\chi|\psi}$
\end_inset

 using the rules of linear algebra
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\braket{\chi|\psi} & =\left(\ket{\chi}\right)^{\dagger}\ket{\psi}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
x_{0}^{\ast} & x_{1}^{\ast}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{array}\right]\nonumber \\
 & =x_{0}^{\ast}c_{0}+x_{1}^{\ast}c_{1}\label{eq:InnerProductTwoStateGeneral}
\end{align}

\end_inset

We can see that that 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\chi|\psi}$
\end_inset

 evaluates to a complex number,
 and we can show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\chi|\psi}\neq\braket{\psi|\chi}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\chi}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\psi}$
\end_inset

 are complex vectors
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This is just like the inner product we're used to in 2D or 3D real-space (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0},x_{1},c_{0},c_{1}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\braket{\chi|\psi} & =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
x_{0}^{\ast} & x_{1}^{\ast}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
x_{0} & x_{1}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{array}\right]=x_{0}c_{0}+x_{1}c_{1}\\
 & =\braket{\psi|\chi}\qquad\left(\text{when }\ket{\psi},\ket{\chi}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
When we take an inner product of a state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 with itself,
 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\braket{\psi|\psi}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
c_{0}^{\ast} & c_{1}^{\ast}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{array}\right]=c_{0}^{\ast}c_{0}+c_{1}^{\ast}c_{1}=\left|c_{0}\right|^{2}+\left|c_{1}\right|^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Notice that we get the sum of probabilities,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|c_{0}\right|^{2}+\left|c_{1}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 so another way to write the normalization condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\braket{\psi|\psi}=1\label{eq:NormalizationCondition-v01}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Again,
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\braket{\psi|\psi}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
c_{0} & c_{1}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{array}\right]=c_{0}^{2}+c_{1}^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If we take the square root of 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{\psi|\psi}$
\end_inset

,
 then we have something that looks like the Pythagorean theorem:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{\braket{\psi|\psi}}=\sqrt{c_{0}^{2}+c_{1}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Indeed for vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{\braket{\psi|\psi}}$
\end_inset

 is the length of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
More generally (i.e.,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 has a dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d>3$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 is a complex vector),
 we say that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{\braket{\psi|\psi}}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\series bold
norm
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection
Normalization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
